    <p>When we multiply a number by $$1,$$ the result is the same number. What happens when we multiply a number by$$-1?$$ Let’s multiply a positive number and then a negative number by $$-1$$ to see what we get.</p>
    <equation>$$\begin{matrix} {-1 \cdot 4} &amp; \qquad &amp; {-1(-3)} \\ -4 &amp; \qquad &amp; 3 \\ {-4\ \text{is the opposite of}\ \textbf{4}} &amp; \qquad &amp; {\textbf{3}\ \text{is the opposite of}\ -3} \end{matrix}$$</equation>
    <p>Each time we multiply a number by $$-1,$$ we get its opposite.</p>
    <note type="Multiplication by−1−1">
      <p>Multiplying a number by $$-1$$ gives its opposite.</p>
      <equation>$$-1a = - a$$</equation>
    </note>
    <example number="3.48">
      <problem>
        <p>Multiply each of the following:</p>
        <list type="ol" style="1">
          <item>ⓐ $$\ -1 \cdot 7\quad$$</item>
          <item>ⓑ $$\ -1(-11)$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Explains properties of negative numbers, covering multiplication with different signs and opposites, with mathematical examples.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓐ</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The signs are different, so the product will be negative.</cell>
              <cell>$$-1 \cdot 7$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Notice that −7 is the opposite of 7.</cell>
              <cell>$$-7$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <table summary="Demonstrates rules for multiplying negative numbers, showing negative times negative equals positive, and the concept of opposites.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓑ</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The signs are the same, so the product will be positive.</cell>
              <cell>$$-1(-11)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Notice that 11 is the opposite of −11.</cell>
              <cell>$$11$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="3.95">
